# Prospective inert file lifetime admission

Owner: `HIAL-VIAL-VERIFICATION-FIXTURE-ARCHITECTURE.19.2.3.1.2.2.5.5.2.3.1.2.2.3.2.15.7.3.12.4.1.3.6.3.1`.
Execution belongs only to `.3.6.3.2`, after admission is committed. This is a
working design, not an execution receipt or a completed admission.

Use the complete model domain committed at
`f451fda60b2761441deb3429bef6aac5852c458f`. Select a bounded representative
subset explicitly, with both image profiles, runtime-first and observation-first
release, archival before/after retirement, and different-image maintenance
before the unrelated runtime retires. Preserve exact membership in the original
ordering domain; do not claim that a selected physical subset executes every
modeled ordering.

Construct new exclusive inert case roots, two private workspaces and one evidence
directory per case. All roots must use the existing admitted repository-relative
namespaces and normal-user, same-volume ownership. Never use the model's
synthetic root spellings as actual allocations. Reuse retained inert payload
bytes as data; never compile or execute them. Supply explicitly synthetic births,
work tokens and settled cleanup records, without claiming native authentication.

Call the unchanged public inactive acquisition, reuse, archive, retirement,
release and explicit-maintenance entrypoints from a new harness. Do not execute
an old qualifier or fixture producer. The existing `FixtureClone.pm` is a pure
JSON/octet clone helper, not an allocation helper. Existing release trace modules
are operation-specific and must not be treated as general lifetime observers.

A new observer must capture actual successful descriptor ownership at opening,
including lower-reader acquisition before a later validation could throw. Keep
actual close outcomes, descriptor absence and any separate recovery distinct.
Preserve errno even when a successful call leaves it nonzero; nonzero errno alone
is not a failed call. Compile hooks before the target modules and retain original
call context and argument aliases. No injected failures belong in this complete
lifetime stage; interruption controls remain separately owned by `.3.6.4`.

Independently snapshot exact owned tree membership, file bytes and stable native
identities before and after each selected complete operation. Directory times or
link counts can change with valid child creation/removal; do not confuse those
with replacement of an unchanged directory inode. Compare expected paths from
completed-event sets, and compare unchanged file identities/bytes independently.
An archive for the second observer may reuse existing content files while
returning its own differently keyed receipt; distinguish logical receipt
creation from physical file creation. A complete image includes its manifest,
not only the selected C/image pair.

Retain a journal before allocation and every first outcome before cleanup. Stop
starting cases after the first unexpected result. Bound nodes, bytes, handles,
output and wall time prospectively. Cleanup only exact newly owned roots after
independent closure, identity and membership checks. Preserve uncertain objects
and primary errors. Keep pre-cleanup observed effects and any separate cleanup
or handle recovery visible. Never silently retry a case or relabel an earlier
failure as successful. Source-only admission must not invoke lifecycle filesystem
operations or allocate fixture roots.

Current source facts: observation wrappers require mode 0700; canonical C and
records require 0400 and inert images 0500. Runtime retirement takes a cleanup
object with compiler, outer and stages. Release takes original acquisition,
archive, selection and settlement. Maintenance is explicitly image-specific.
The permanent lock remains named until the new harness performs its separately
recorded final cleanup. These source facts must be pinned and checked by the
prospective admission rather than inferred from model-only identities.
